\chapter{The C Library: musl on Ulix}
\label{ch:libc}

Until now the user programs of the port are small and freestanding: the 59 tools of the book use the book's own library
(\texttt{ulixlib}) and the test programs use a tiny \texttt{ulib}. For real programs, and later for the C++ library, an editor, an
interpreter or a browser, the system needs the C library every Unix program expects. The choice is \emph{musl} (MIT licence): small
enough to read, written without the history of glibc, and good for static linking. musl comes as an unmodified submodule
(\texttt{third\_party/musl}, tag v1.2.6, see \texttt{third\_party/README.md}); everything on this page is our part: the system call
layer that connects musl to the kernel, the start-up information a program finds on its stack, and the memory, time and signal
calls that musl needs.

\section{How musl attaches to the kernel}

musl talks to its kernel through one inline function per number of arguments (\texttt{\_\_syscall0} to \texttt{\_\_syscall6},
\texttt{arch/aarch64/syscall\_arch.h}): the \emph{Linux/AArch64 convention} --- number in \texttt{x8}, arguments in \texttt{x0} to
\texttt{x5}, \texttt{svc 0}, result in \texttt{x0}, and an error is returned as a negative error number. Ulix has a different
convention (number in \texttt{x0}, arguments in \texttt{x1} to \texttt{x5}, result \texttt{-1} and the error number kept in the
process), with the numbers of the book, which are those of Linux/i386. The two conventions cannot be told apart from a single
\texttt{svc}: \texttt{x0} is the number in one and the first argument in the other.

Three ways were considered (the decision is recorded in issue \#27 and in \texttt{ARM64-PORT.md}):

\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Change musl} (\texttt{syscall\_arch.h}) so that it uses the Ulix convention. This is the translation table ``in
  \texttt{arch/aarch64}''. It makes the submodule a fork and every musl update a merge, and the translation of structures and
  64-bit results would still be needed.
\item \emph{Renumber the kernel} to the Linux/AArch64 numbers. All existing programs, the book's \texttt{ulixlib} and the
  tests would have to be rebuilt, and the numbers of the book (which the students read) would disappear from the port.
\item \emph{A second system call interface in the kernel}, chosen per program. The kernel stays as it is for the old programs and
  speaks Linux/AArch64 to the programs that musl built. musl stays unmodified, and the translation is in one place, in our
  code, readable next to the kernel functions it calls.
\end{enumerate}

We take the third way. A program tells which interface it wants in its ELF header: the byte \texttt{EI\_OSABI} is
\texttt{ELFOSABI\_LINUX} (3). The linker does not write that byte, so the build sets it after linking
(\texttt{tools/mark\_musl.py}). The loader (\texttt{elf.c}) remembers it, \texttt{linux\_abi\_as[]} keeps it for the address space
(copied by \texttt{fork}, replaced by \texttt{execve}), and \texttt{book\_syscall} sends the call to \texttt{linux\_syscall} instead of
the book's dispatcher.

The function \texttt{linux\_syscall} is the translation: it looks at \texttt{x8}, and for most calls it moves the arguments into the
registers the book's handler (\texttt{syscall\_read}, \texttt{syscall\_write}, \ldots) expects and calls that handler. A few calls
need real work because the kernel has nothing similar yet: \texttt{mmap}, \texttt{mprotect} and friends (this chapter), the clocks,
the signal calls. What is missing is answered with \texttt{ENOSYS}, and the kernel prints the number once, so that a program that
needs more shows exactly what.

The numbers below are the Linux/AArch64 numbers as they are listed in musl's own header (\texttt{bits/syscall.h.in}); they are facts
about an interface, no Linux code is used.

