\section{Python: CPython on Ulix}
\label{sec:libc:python}

The aim of the epic ``Python'' (issue \#7) is that \texttt{python3} runs on Ulix. The interpreter is CPython 3.12.7, an unmodified submodule
(\texttt{third\_party/cpython}, licence check in \texttt{third\_party/LICENCE-CHECK-cpython.md}), built \emph{statically} against musl. This section is the
log of how it is attached; the files of this part are not literate sources (they are build scripts and data, like \texttt{libc/Makefile}):

\begin{description}
\item[\texttt{libc/musl-cc}] A shell script that calls \texttt{aarch64-elf-gcc} with the headers, the start files and \texttt{libc.a} of the musl
  that \texttt{libc/Makefile} installed (\texttt{libc/build/sysroot}), and with nothing of the host. CPython's \texttt{configure} wants a compiler that
  can compile \emph{and link} a test program, and this one can; it also links with \texttt{libc/musl.ld} when \texttt{MUSL\_LDSCRIPT} says so.
\item[\texttt{libc/python-setup.local}] The list of extension modules. There is no \texttt{dlopen} (issue \#40: static linking only, see
  below), so every module that the interpreter should have is compiled into it. \texttt{Modules/Setup.bootstrap} of CPython already
  has the ones needed to start (\texttt{posix}, \texttt{\_io}, \texttt{\_sre}, \texttt{time}, \texttt{\_thread}, \ldots); the file adds \texttt{math},
  \texttt{\_struct}, \texttt{array}, \texttt{binascii}, \texttt{\_json}, \texttt{select} and a few more. A module that needs a library that musl does not have
  (zlib, OpenSSL, SQLite) is not in the list.
\item[\texttt{libc/Makefile}] \texttt{make PYTHON=1} configures CPython out of the tree (\texttt{libc/build/python}) with
  \texttt{--host=aarch64-linux-musl --disable-shared --without-ensurepip}, builds \texttt{python.exe}, strips it and marks it for the kernel
  (\texttt{mark\_musl.py}). CPython builds itself in two steps (it runs a small interpreter on the build machine to freeze the
  modules \texttt{os}, \texttt{site}, \texttt{io}, \ldots\ into the executable), so a \texttt{python3.12} must be on the host (\texttt{BUILD\_PYTHON}).
  The default is on when the submodule is checked out; \texttt{make PYTHON=0} leaves it out and it is not even compiled.
\item[\texttt{/musl/python}, \texttt{/usr/local/lib/python3.12/encodings/}] The disk gets the interpreter (7.4 MByte, stripped) and the part of
  the standard library that start-up needs: the \texttt{encodings} package. Everything else that \texttt{import} may ask for comes from the frozen
  modules inside the executable. More of \texttt{Lib/} is a matter of adding file names to \texttt{PYTHON\_LIB\_FILES} in the \texttt{Makefile}.
\end{description}

\paragraph{What the first runs showed} (this is the ``spike'', issue \#57; every line is something that really happened, in this order):
\begin{enumerate}
\item It compiled and linked on the first try (no patch, 4.4 MByte of code, 1.4 MByte of data). The default linker script put the program headers
  outside the loaded image, so musl's start-up code (\texttt{AT\_PHDR}) failed; with \texttt{libc/musl.ld} it starts. The script got a \texttt{PT\_TLS}
  segment for the thread-local variables of CPython (\texttt{\_Py\_thread\_local}) and of musl.
\item \texttt{Fatal Python error: \_PyErr\_NoMemory}: the second \texttt{mmap} of the same size failed. The search for a free address range of
  \texttt{linux\_mmap} returned the \emph{lowest} page of the run it had found and the function then subtracted the length of the run once more,
  so the new mapping overlapped the pages below it. The test program of musl only ever mapped into free space and never saw it.
\item \texttt{ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'encodings'}: the standard library has to be on the disk. The disk image of the book (1.4 MByte,
  480 inodes, the zone map in one block) is too small for a 7 MByte file. The Makefile now makes a new file system of 16 MByte and copies the files of
  the book's image into it (\texttt{tools/minix\_copy.py}; \texttt{minix\_add.py} learned to write files with a double indirect block, the kernel could
  read them already). One thing about the book had to be respected: \texttt{mx\_unlink} frees a zone with the constant \texttt{33}, the number of
  the \emph{first data zone minus one} of the floppy image, so the new file system must also start its data at zone 34. It has 464 inodes
  and a zone map of two blocks for that (\texttt{minix\_mkfs.py --inodes=464}).
\item A fault at an address 64 KByte below the stack pointer: CPython's parser has a function with a 64 KByte array. The stack grew by one page
  when the page just below it was touched, as in the book; now it grows by as many pages as needed if the address is at most 1 MByte below (an
  access further away is still a wild pointer and kills the process, as before).
\item \texttt{renameat} (38) was missing: the interpreter writes compiled modules under a temporary name and renames them (now implemented
  with \texttt{u\_link} and \texttt{u\_unlink}, see above), and a call to \texttt{socket} (198) that is answered with \texttt{ENOSYS} (the sockets for
  programs are the next chapter's work); the interpreter does not need either to go on.
\item After that: \texttt{print (6 * 7)}, a script with the \texttt{math} module, \texttt{os.getcwd ()}, sets and sorting run, and the exit status is right.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{The decision for \#40 (\texttt{dlopen}).} Python extension modules are usually shared objects, loaded with \texttt{dlopen}. Ulix does not
have a dynamic linker (musl's static \texttt{dlopen} answers ``Dynamic loading not supported''); it would need a \texttt{PT\_INTERP} loader, position independent
code, relocations and file mappings in the kernel. All modules that are wanted are linked in instead (list above). Python's \texttt{ctypes}
and third-party packages with C code are out for now. The decision is revisited together with file mappings (not before they exist).

\paragraph{The decision for \#57 (MicroPython or CPython).} Both were looked at. MicroPython has no standard library to speak of and a different
set of modules; programs for ``Python 3'' that the user will expect (the \texttt{json}, \texttt{re}, \texttt{socket} modules of the standard library) are CPython programs.
CPython ran on the first attempt without a patch, and what it needs from the kernel (see the numbered list) is what any C program that uses
threads, sockets and files needs; that work is useful for every other program. So CPython it is. The price is size: 7.4 MByte on the disk instead of a few
hundred KByte, and the 16 MByte root file system.

\paragraph{Test.} \texttt{tools/python\_test.py} (\texttt{make python-test}, job \texttt{python} of \texttt{make pcheck}) boots the shell kernel with a disk that has
the interpreter, logs in and runs \texttt{python -c print(6*7)} and the script \texttt{/musl/hello.py}; the output is compared line by line. It prints
a message and succeeds if the build has no interpreter (\texttt{PYTHON=0}).
\section{Test of sockets: \texttt{sockettest}}
\label{src:libc/sockettest.c}

The program talks to two servers that \texttt{tools/socket\_test.py} runs on the host (qemu's user network makes the host \texttt{10.0.2.2}): a TCP server that answers
every line in capital letters and closes when the client has finished sending, and a UDP server that does the same for every datagram. It is started
as \texttt{sockettest 10.0.2.2 TCPPORT UDPPORT}. The checks go through the ways in which the calls are used by real programs: blocking and non-blocking sockets, \texttt{poll} and \texttt{select}
with a pipe and a socket, an error from \texttt{connect}, and \texttt{getaddrinfo} with names from \texttt{/etc/hosts}.

<<libc/sockettest.c>>=
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <errno.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <poll.h>
#include <netdb.h>
#include <arpa/inet.h>
#include <sys/select.h>
#include <sys/socket.h>
#include <netinet/in.h>

static int fails = 0;
#define CHECK(c, msg) do { if (c) printf ("  user ok:   %s\n", msg); \
                           else { printf ("  user FAIL: %s\n", msg); fails++; } } while (0)

static struct sockaddr_in address (const char *ip, int port) {
  struct sockaddr_in a;
  memset (&a, 0, sizeof a);
  a.sin_family = AF_INET;  a.sin_port = htons (port);  inet_pton (AF_INET, ip, &a.sin_addr);
  return a;
}

int main (int argc, char **argv) {
  if (argc != 4) { printf ("usage: sockettest HOST TCPPORT UDPPORT\n");  return 2; }
  struct sockaddr_in tcp = address (argv[1], atoi (argv[2])), udp = address (argv[1], atoi (argv[3])), from;
  socklen_t fromlen = sizeof from;
  char buf[200];

  errno = 0;
  CHECK (socket (AF_INET6, SOCK_STREAM, 0) == -1 && errno == EAFNOSUPPORT, "socket (AF_INET6) is refused with EAFNOSUPPORT");

  // datagrams: sendto and recvfrom, with the address of the sender
  int u = socket (AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM, 0);
  struct pollfd pfd = { u, POLLIN, 0 };
  CHECK (u >= 0 && sendto (u, "ping", 4, 0, (struct sockaddr *) &udp, sizeof udp) == 4, "UDP: sendto a datagram to the host server");
  CHECK (poll (&pfd, 1, 3000) == 1 && (pfd.revents & POLLIN), "poll: the answer makes the socket readable");
  int n = recvfrom (u, buf, sizeof buf, 0, (struct sockaddr *) &from, &fromlen);
  CHECK (n == 4 && !memcmp (buf, "PING", 4) && from.sin_port == udp.sin_port, "UDP: recvfrom gives PING and the port of the server");
  CHECK (poll (&pfd, 1, 100) == 0, "poll with nothing to read: returns 0 after the timeout (100 ms)");
  int nb = recv (u, buf, sizeof buf, MSG_DONTWAIT);
  CHECK (nb == -1 && errno == EAGAIN, "recv (MSG_DONTWAIT) with nothing there: EAGAIN");
  CHECK (connect (u, (struct sockaddr *) &udp, sizeof udp) == 0 && send (u, "pong", 4, 0) == 4 && recv (u, buf, sizeof buf, 0) == 4 && !memcmp (buf, "PONG", 4), "UDP: connect, send and recv");
  struct sockaddr_in me;  socklen_t melen = sizeof me;
  CHECK (getsockname (u, (struct sockaddr *) &me, &melen) == 0 && ntohs (me.sin_port) >= 1024, "getsockname: the socket got a local port");
  close (u);

  // a stream: connect, write, read, shutdown, the end of the stream
  int s = socket (AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, 0);
  CHECK (s >= 0 && connect (s, (struct sockaddr *) &tcp, sizeof tcp) == 0, "TCP: connect to the host server");
  struct sockaddr_in peer;  socklen_t peerlen = sizeof peer;
  CHECK (getpeername (s, (struct sockaddr *) &peer, &peerlen) == 0 && peer.sin_port == tcp.sin_port, "getpeername: the port of the server");
  CHECK (write (s, "hello\n", 6) == 6, "write 6 bytes to the stream");
  pfd.fd = s;
  CHECK (poll (&pfd, 1, 3000) == 1 && (pfd.revents & POLLIN), "poll: the answer makes the stream readable");
  n = read (s, buf, sizeof buf);
  CHECK (n == 6 && !memcmp (buf, "HELLO\n", 6), "read gives HELLO");
  CHECK (shutdown (s, SHUT_WR) == 0, "shutdown (SHUT_WR)");
  n = read (s, buf, sizeof buf);
  CHECK (n == 0, "after the server has closed: read returns 0 (end of the stream)");
  close (s);

  // a connection that is refused
  struct sockaddr_in closed = address (argv[1], 1);
  s = socket (AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, 0);
  errno = 0;
  CHECK (connect (s, (struct sockaddr *) &closed, sizeof closed) == -1 && errno == ECONNREFUSED, "connect to a port where nobody listens: ECONNREFUSED");
  close (s);

  // non-blocking connect: EINPROGRESS, then poll says writable
  s = socket (AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM | SOCK_NONBLOCK, 0);
  errno = 0;
  int c = connect (s, (struct sockaddr *) &tcp, sizeof tcp);
  pfd.fd = s;  pfd.events = POLLOUT;
  CHECK (c == -1 && errno == EINPROGRESS && poll (&pfd, 1, 5000) == 1 && (pfd.revents & POLLOUT), "non-blocking connect: EINPROGRESS, then poll reports POLLOUT");
  errno = 0;
  CHECK (read (s, buf, sizeof buf) == -1 && errno == EAGAIN, "read on a non-blocking stream with nothing there: EAGAIN");
  close (s);

  // select () with a pipe: not ready, then ready
  int p[2];
  pipe (p);
  fd_set rs;
  struct timeval tv = { 0, 50000 };
  FD_ZERO (&rs);  FD_SET (p[0], &rs);
  CHECK (select (p[0] + 1, &rs, 0, 0, &tv) == 0, "select on an empty pipe: nothing is ready after 50 ms");
  write (p[1], "x", 1);
  FD_ZERO (&rs);  FD_SET (p[0], &rs);
  tv.tv_usec = 50000;
  CHECK (select (p[0] + 1, &rs, 0, 0, &tv) == 1 && FD_ISSET (p[0], &rs), "select: after a write the read end of the pipe is ready");
  struct pollfd two[2] = { { p[0], POLLIN, 0 }, { p[1], POLLOUT, 0 } };
  CHECK (poll (two, 2, 0) == 2 && (two[0].revents & POLLIN) && (two[1].revents & POLLOUT), "poll on both ends of a pipe: readable and writable");
  close (p[0]);  close (p[1]);

  // getaddrinfo with the names in /etc/hosts (and numbers)
  struct addrinfo hints, *res = 0;
  memset (&hints, 0, sizeof hints);
  hints.ai_family = AF_INET;  hints.ai_socktype = SOCK_STREAM;
  int g = getaddrinfo ("localhost", "80", &hints, &res);
  CHECK (g == 0 && res && ((struct sockaddr_in *) res->ai_addr)->sin_addr.s_addr == htonl (INADDR_LOOPBACK) && ((struct sockaddr_in *) res->ai_addr)->sin_port == htons (80), "getaddrinfo (\"localhost\", \"80\"): 127.0.0.1, port 80 (from /etc/hosts)");
  if (res) freeaddrinfo (res);
  g = getaddrinfo ("10.0.2.2", 0, &hints, &res);
  CHECK (g == 0 && res && ((struct sockaddr_in *) res->ai_addr)->sin_addr.s_addr == inet_addr ("10.0.2.2"), "getaddrinfo of a numeric address");
  if (res) freeaddrinfo (res);
  g = getaddrinfo ("gateway.ulix", 0, &hints, &res);
  CHECK (g == 0 && res && ((struct sockaddr_in *) res->ai_addr)->sin_addr.s_addr == inet_addr ("10.0.2.2"), "getaddrinfo (\"gateway.ulix\"): 10.0.2.2 (from /etc/hosts)");
  if (res) freeaddrinfo (res);
  return fails;
}
